1
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:04,030
Hello, English learners.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:06,280
Welcome back to EnglishPod.

3
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:06,750
My name is Marco.

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:08,460
And I'm Erika.

5
00:00:08,460 --> 00:00:13,210
And today we're bringing you part two of the presidential farewell speech of President Eisenhower.

6
00:00:13,810 --> 00:00:14,170
That's right.

7
00:00:14,390 --> 00:00:17,070
So, we saw in a previous lesson the beginning of this speech.

8
00:00:17,310 --> 00:00:19,650
Now we're going to look towards the end of the speech.

9
00:00:19,870 --> 00:00:23,290
Now, we must tell you as well that we did change the speech a little bit, right?

10
00:00:23,530 --> 00:00:28,390
We changed it a little bit to make it a little bit more comprehensible and also we did shorten it.

11
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:30,440
We didn't make it this short.

12
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:32,110
We did take out a lot of stuff.

13
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:32,680
All right.

14
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:35,700
So, again, Marco, why are we looking at this speech?

15
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:36,880
What are we learning from it?

16
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:44,420
Well, in this last part of the presidential farewell speech, there are a lot of great words that are more difficult and very poetic.

17
00:00:44,760 --> 00:00:49,640
So you can use them in different formal circumstances or just to sound a little bit more well-spoken.

18
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:50,640
Yeah.

19
00:00:50,640 --> 00:01:03,760
And we also, again, are going to look at some really quite difficult and challenging structures that we hope you can use maybe when you're giving a formal talk or even in your writing if you want to sound quite professional.

20
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:04,500
Okay.

21
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:05,000
Very good.

22
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:08,780
So, why don't we listen to the dialogue for the first time?

23
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:13,660
Let's listen how President Eisenhower says goodbye and then we'll come back and look at the words.

24
00:01:19,770 --> 00:01:25,490
We now stand ten years past the midpoint of a century that has witnessed four major wars among great nations.

25
00:01:26,380 --> 00:01:28,400
Three of these involved our own country.

26
00:01:29,290 --> 00:01:37,170
Despite the carnage of these conflicts, America is today the strongest, the most influential, and the most productive nation in the world.

27
00:01:38,130 --> 00:01:54,290
We are understandably proud of this preeminence, yet we realize that America's leadership and prestige depend not merely upon our unmatched material progress, riches, and military strength, but on how we use our power in the interests of world peace and human betterment.

28
00:01:54,710 --> 00:02:07,510
Throughout America's adventure in free government, such basic purposes have been to keep the peace, to foster progress and human achievement, and to enhance liberty, dignity, and integrity among peoples and among nations.

29
00:02:09,070 --> 00:02:47,590
We pray that peoples of all faiths, all races, all nations may have their great human needs satisfied, that those now denied opportunity shall come to enjoy it to the full, that all who yearn for freedom may experience its spiritual blessings, that those who have freedom will understand also its heavy responsibilities, that all who are insensitive to the needs of others will learn charity, that the scourges of poverty, disease, and ignorance will be made to disappear from the earth, and that in the goodness of time, all peoples will come to live together in a peace guaranteed by the binding force of mutual respect and love.

30
00:02:48,470 --> 00:02:51,190
Now, on Friday noon, I am to become a private citizen.

31
00:02:51,900 --> 00:02:53,160
I am proud to do so.

32
00:02:53,680 --> 00:02:55,440
I look forward to it.

33
00:02:55,440 --> 00:02:56,440
Thank you and good night.

34
00:02:58,830 --> 00:03:01,950
Well, that certainly was a very moving speech, hey?

35
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:06,050
Very moving and to a certain point, a little bit religious, huh?

36
00:03:06,410 --> 00:03:11,590
Yeah, I think we'll talk a little bit about that a little bit later, but let's concentrate on the language now.

37
00:03:11,850 --> 00:03:13,290
So we'll go to Language Takeaway.

38
00:03:14,950 --> 00:03:15,890
Language Takeaway.

39
00:03:17,190 --> 00:03:23,440
All right, on Language Takeaway today, we'll start with our first word and it's preeminence.

40
00:03:24,050 --> 00:03:27,090
All right, we are understandably proud of this preeminence.

41
00:03:27,410 --> 00:03:28,850
A preeminence.

42
00:03:29,490 --> 00:03:31,450
So preeminence, what is this?

43
00:03:31,950 --> 00:03:34,970
Basically, it's the state of being the leader of something.

44
00:03:35,290 --> 00:03:40,050
Okay, so in this case, America was the preeminent nation.

45
00:03:40,690 --> 00:03:45,930
Right, so they were coming up, they were very prosperous, so they had a preeminence in the world.

46
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:57,280
All right, so I think we can see here, you can use this as an adjective, a preeminent nation, or as a noun, an uncountable noun, the preeminence of our state.

47
00:03:57,280 --> 00:03:57,510
Very good.

48
00:03:58,280 --> 00:04:05,420
Since we can use this word as a noun and an adjective, why don't we listen to some more examples so we can get a better understanding of it?

49
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:07,270
Example one.

50
00:04:09,450 --> 00:04:14,490
Now, please welcome Dr. Frank Hicks, the world's most preeminent expert on nuclear biology.

51
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:17,000
Example two.

52
00:04:19,350 --> 00:04:24,370
He's been a leader in his field for 25 years and his preeminence has never been questioned.

53
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:26,980
Example three.

54
00:04:28,930 --> 00:04:32,310
Carol Dixon is one of the most preeminent experts in green technology.

55
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:45,100
All right, well, Eisenhower was talking about all of the things that the American people had done since they became a free nation, and one of these things was to foster progress in human achievement.

56
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:48,320
Foster progress in human achievement.

57
00:04:48,760 --> 00:04:51,480
Well, I like this verb a lot, to foster something.

58
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:55,960
So if I say to foster progress or to foster something, what am I saying?

59
00:04:56,680 --> 00:04:58,660
To help something grow and develop.

60
00:04:59,040 --> 00:05:00,900
To take care of it, to nurse it.

61
00:05:01,280 --> 00:05:02,280
Yeah.

62
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:05,760
And we commonly hear this word associated with children, right?

63
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:06,540
A foster child.

64
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:06,880
Yeah.

65
00:05:07,020 --> 00:05:07,640
So what's that?

66
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:15,360
If you have a foster child, you are basically taking care of a child because the parents can't look after them.

67
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:19,000
It's not like adopting because you don't actually, it's not actually legal.

68
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,330
It's just that you're helping somebody out by raising this child.

69
00:05:21,520 --> 00:05:22,520
Right.

70
00:05:22,520 --> 00:05:25,960
So we can see the connection there, a foster child to foster something.

71
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:30,800
But you know, this is really a great word and I think it would help to give a couple of examples of how we could use it.

72
00:05:30,830 --> 00:05:36,830
So for example, I can say, our company is looking to foster better creativity in the workplace.

73
00:05:37,450 --> 00:05:43,210
Or we could say, here at Praxis Language, we foster the development of your English.

74
00:05:43,610 --> 00:05:44,270
Very good.

75
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:46,170
So the word foster.

76
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:57,440
Now moving on, in his speech, he was talking about nations and faiths and different types of groups and he said, all who yearn for freedom.

77
00:05:57,940 --> 00:05:58,240
All right.

78
00:05:58,880 --> 00:05:59,640
This is a great phrase.

79
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:01,920
All who yearn for freedom.

80
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:03,820
To yearn.

81
00:06:04,160 --> 00:06:05,220
So to yearn for something.

82
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:11,920
Now if I yearn for freedom or I yearn for something, it's basically that I really want it in my heart.

83
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:16,420
Yeah, you really wish for it in a way that's almost, almost painful.

84
00:06:17,160 --> 00:06:18,940
You know, you want it so badly.

85
00:06:19,580 --> 00:06:23,040
Now you wouldn't say like, I'm yearning for ice cream, would you?

86
00:06:23,220 --> 00:06:27,920
No, unless you were locked up in prison and hadn't had ice cream for 55 years.

87
00:06:28,260 --> 00:06:28,640
Exactly.

88
00:06:29,400 --> 00:06:35,080
So it's usually related to something that's very difficult to get or something like freedom or yearning for.

89
00:06:35,080 --> 00:06:35,580
That's really important.

90
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:36,280
Exactly.

91
00:06:37,080 --> 00:06:37,420
Something very important.

92
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:42,680
And now for our last word, President Eisenhower was talking about charity.

93
00:06:43,240 --> 00:06:47,840
He was talking about freedom and he mentioned the scourges of poverty.

94
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:48,360
Okay.

95
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:49,460
So scourges.

96
00:06:50,300 --> 00:06:51,840
The scourges of poverty.

97
00:06:53,400 --> 00:06:56,720
Scourges is a really difficult but interesting word.

98
00:06:56,720 --> 00:07:00,960
So why don't we hear a few more examples to help us understand it.

99
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:01,230
Example one.

100
00:07:02,580 --> 00:07:06,080
Malaria is a disease that continues to be a scourge to the developing world.

101
00:07:07,390 --> 00:07:08,070
Example two.

102
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:14,260
One day we hope to eliminate the scourge of cancer.

103
00:07:14,260 --> 00:07:16,640
Example three.

104
00:07:16,640 --> 00:07:21,420
NATO promises to wipe out the scourge of terrorism.

105
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:27,420
So as we can see, the scourges of something basically is the cause of pain or suffering.

106
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:32,060
So now relating it back to the text, we see scourges of poverty.

107
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:36,080
Yeah, scourges of poverty, scourges of disease, and scourges of ignorance.

108
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:37,080
All right.

109
00:07:37,080 --> 00:07:42,900
So we can see that poverty, ignorance, and disease cause a lot of really terrible things, right?

110
00:07:43,880 --> 00:07:44,880
Exactly.

111
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:45,880
So that's why we say they are scourges.

112
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:46,220
Okay.

113
00:07:46,640 --> 00:07:47,320
So scourges.

114
00:07:47,880 --> 00:07:51,760
So that brings us to the end of our words for takeaway today.

115
00:07:51,760 --> 00:07:59,360
But as we talked about earlier, we've got some really difficult and interesting structures to look at now in Fluency Builder.

116
00:07:59,360 --> 00:07:59,660
Fluency Builder.

117
00:08:00,360 --> 00:08:00,810
Okay.

118
00:08:01,360 --> 00:08:06,050
So in Fluency Builder, we'll start off with the very first sentence of the paragraph.

119
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:11,610
When he said, we now stand 10 years past the midpoint of a century.

120
00:08:11,990 --> 00:08:12,250
Okay.

121
00:08:12,270 --> 00:08:13,310
So I love this phrase.

122
00:08:13,530 --> 00:08:16,470
We now stand 10 years past the midpoint of the century.

123
00:08:16,890 --> 00:08:21,510
Now it's interesting because he is making reference to the future in the present.

124
00:08:23,320 --> 00:08:25,080
And he's using a great phrase to do that.

125
00:08:25,080 --> 00:08:27,110
We now stand.

126
00:08:27,860 --> 00:08:34,490
Now we can change it a little bit and use the first part, we now stand in different circumstances.

127
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:38,600
So why don't we listen to a couple of examples.

128
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:41,040
Example one.

129
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:46,700
We now stand before you to declare our love and be united in marriage.

130
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:46,950
Example two.

131
00:08:48,760 --> 00:08:53,440
I stand before this court and declare my innocence.

132
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:53,790
Example three.

133
00:08:56,040 --> 00:09:00,170
Together we now stand before this great challenge and we will fight arm in arm to defeat poverty.

134
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:01,960
Right.

135
00:09:02,470 --> 00:09:07,470
So you can see that this is a wonderful phrase to use maybe in a formal speech, right?

136
00:09:07,630 --> 00:09:07,770
Right.

137
00:09:07,950 --> 00:09:09,830
Or a wedding again or a formal ceremony.

138
00:09:10,350 --> 00:09:10,470
Yeah.

139
00:09:11,130 --> 00:09:20,910
So, and it gives you this idea, this sort of very poetic image of people sort of almost standing at the cliff, standing before the, you know, some big change that's about to take place.

140
00:09:21,190 --> 00:09:21,530
Exactly.

141
00:09:22,250 --> 00:09:27,870
So 10 years past the midpoint of a century, the United States has witnessed four major wars, right?

142
00:09:28,170 --> 00:09:28,430
Okay.

143
00:09:28,890 --> 00:09:38,110
And well, he said, despite the carnage of these conflicts, America is today the strongest, the most influential and most productive nation in the world.

144
00:09:38,650 --> 00:09:38,810
Okay.

145
00:09:38,910 --> 00:09:40,950
So a great, great, great structure here.

146
00:09:41,600 --> 00:09:46,640
Despite the carnage of these conflicts, America today is the strongest blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

147
00:09:46,640 --> 00:09:47,640
Right.

148
00:09:47,640 --> 00:09:50,240
Now, let's look at that first word, despite the carnage of these conflicts.

149
00:09:50,510 --> 00:09:50,670
Okay.

150
00:09:51,240 --> 00:09:53,240
So, carnage, what's that?

151
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:54,510
Carnage is like a massacre, right?

152
00:09:54,720 --> 00:09:56,150
Where many people got hurt or killed.

153
00:09:56,410 --> 00:09:56,670
Okay.

154
00:09:56,690 --> 00:09:59,870
So a pretty violent situation when a lot of people died.

155
00:10:00,920 --> 00:10:02,730
And now why does he use this word despite?

156
00:10:03,210 --> 00:10:03,470
Okay.

157
00:10:03,470 --> 00:10:14,070
So he's basically saying even though we had all of this death and violence in this century, we're still the greatest nation, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

158
00:10:14,990 --> 00:10:15,270
Exactly.

159
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:16,680
That's exactly it.

160
00:10:16,680 --> 00:10:24,480
I can say, for example, despite the economic crisis the world is going through, our company has an enormous opportunity to grow and be more profitable.

161
00:10:24,600 --> 00:10:25,040
Okay.

162
00:10:25,040 --> 00:10:28,760
Or if we want to take it and imagine we're at a wedding, we might say something like this.

163
00:10:29,160 --> 00:10:35,420
You could say, despite the many difficulties this couple has faced, they have a very bright and happy future together.

164
00:10:35,700 --> 00:10:36,240
Very good.

165
00:10:36,620 --> 00:10:42,380
And now if we take a look at the last paragraph, it's very interesting because it's one sentence.

166
00:10:43,020 --> 00:10:44,240
It's a really long sentence.

167
00:10:44,460 --> 00:10:45,440
It's a very long sentence.

168
00:10:45,940 --> 00:10:50,840
So even though it's not a phrase, let's take a look at the punctuation of this paragraph.

169
00:10:51,390 --> 00:10:51,690
Okay.

170
00:10:52,030 --> 00:11:01,230
So Eisenhower starts out by saying, we pray that peoples of all face, all races, all nations may have their great human needs satisfied.

171
00:11:01,690 --> 00:11:03,850
And then we have a semicolon there, right?

172
00:11:04,400 --> 00:11:05,400
Right.

173
00:11:05,400 --> 00:11:05,990
So why do we have a semicolon?

174
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:07,690
Well, here's what he's doing.

175
00:11:08,440 --> 00:11:09,890
He's saying we pray for something.

176
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:15,480
And then he's listing the many different things that he's praying for.

177
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:16,480
Okay.

178
00:11:16,480 --> 00:11:17,450
So it's basically a list.

179
00:11:17,910 --> 00:11:18,050
Yep.

180
00:11:18,880 --> 00:11:19,880
Okay.

181
00:11:19,880 --> 00:11:25,400
So we know that when you're writing a list, we commonly use commas between all the things that are in this list.

182
00:11:25,400 --> 00:11:30,300
But here, because they are long and complex ideas, a comma is not enough.

183
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:32,060
You need a semicolon.

184
00:11:32,420 --> 00:11:32,540
Okay.

185
00:11:32,580 --> 00:11:41,360
So we can see here from the text that the semicolon is a great way to separate ideas, big ideas, complex ideas in a list.

186
00:11:41,820 --> 00:11:42,260
Exactly.

187
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:48,720
So you can use it and not necessarily have to end your sentence or that each idea become one sentence.

188
00:11:49,180 --> 00:11:50,960
You can all just make it one big sentence.

189
00:11:51,360 --> 00:11:52,360
Yep.

190
00:11:52,360 --> 00:12:01,170
You know, and actually I remember back to high school, Marco, my teacher told me, hey, if you throw in a few semicolons into your writing and do it properly, you'll really impress people.

191
00:12:01,680 --> 00:12:02,680
All right.

192
00:12:02,680 --> 00:12:10,910
So this is good advice that you can take and throw in a couple of semicolons, a little bit of commas here and there, and it'll look like you really know how to write.

193
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:12,440
Yep.

194
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:13,440
Okay.

195
00:12:13,440 --> 00:12:24,000
So with that in mind, why don't we listen to what Eisenhower wrote one last time.

196
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:29,600
We now stand 10 years past the midpoint of a century that has witnessed four major wars among great nations.

197
00:12:31,020 --> 00:12:32,450
Three of these involved our own country.

198
00:12:33,880 --> 00:12:41,280
Despite the carnage of these conflicts, America is today the strongest, the most influential and the most productive nation in the world.

199
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:58,410
We are understandably proud of this preeminence, yet we realize that America's leadership and prestige depend not merely upon our unmatched material progress, riches and military strength, but on how we use our power in the interests of world peace and human betterment.

200
00:12:59,400 --> 00:13:11,630
Throughout America's adventure in free government, such basic purposes have been to keep the peace, to foster progress and human achievement, and to enhance liberty, dignity, and integrity among peoples and among nations.

201
00:13:13,320 --> 00:13:52,520
We pray that peoples of all faiths, all races, all nations may have their great human needs satisfied, that those now denied opportunity shall come to enjoy it to the full, that all who yearn for freedom may experience its spiritual blessings, that those who have freedom will understand also its heavy responsibilities, that all who are insensitive to the needs of others will learn charity, that the scourges of poverty, disease, and ignorance will be made to disappear from the earth, and that in the goodness of time, all peoples will come to live together in a peace guaranteed by the binding force of mutual respect and love.

202
00:13:52,520 --> 00:13:55,340
Now, on Friday noon, I am to become a private citizen.

203
00:13:56,280 --> 00:13:57,290
I am proud to do so.

204
00:13:57,850 --> 00:13:58,650
I look forward to it.

205
00:13:59,210 --> 00:14:00,530
Thank you, and good night.

206
00:14:05,920 --> 00:14:11,420
All right, we're back, and we're here again with Pete, our voice actor for the second part of the dialogue.

207
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:14,440
And Pete, we have a couple more questions for you today.

208
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:16,040
Cool.

209
00:14:16,040 --> 00:14:19,940
So I hear in Eisenhower's speech that he keeps referencing God.

210
00:14:20,320 --> 00:14:22,800
Like he keeps saying, I pray, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

211
00:14:22,800 --> 00:14:25,360
He said God's speed, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

212
00:14:25,360 --> 00:14:26,520
So what's up with that?

213
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:27,680
Why is he talking about God?

214
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:29,520
Well, this is a very interesting question.

215
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:34,520
Traditionally, America has had a really clear boundary between church and state.

216
00:14:34,820 --> 00:14:40,140
And you'll hear a lot of rhetoric these days that says, you know, we're a Christian nation, and we were founded as a Christian nation.

217
00:14:40,420 --> 00:14:41,080
Well, that's baloney.

218
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:43,880
Baloney, so it's not true.

219
00:14:43,880 --> 00:14:48,060
At the time when the country was founded 200 years ago, it was overwhelmingly Christian.

220
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:54,420
If the founding fathers, as we call them, like George Washington and them, if they had wanted a Christian nation, they could have had it.

221
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:56,180
They explicitly did not.

222
00:14:57,280 --> 00:14:59,580
And a lot of people since then have gotten that confused.

223
00:14:59,840 --> 00:15:11,480
And now when we have a very large Muslim population, we have Buddhists, we have, you know, people in all various different kinds of, you know, Mormonism and everything, it's even less possible now.

224
00:15:11,480 --> 00:15:14,340
So I'm kind of baffled when I hear people say that America is a Christian nation.

225
00:15:15,200 --> 00:15:18,640
But you even have it now in the coins, like, or on the money, right?

226
00:15:18,720 --> 00:15:20,400
It says in God we trust.

227
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:21,400
Right.

228
00:15:21,400 --> 00:15:23,340
That started in the early 20th century.

229
00:15:23,440 --> 00:15:24,960
I think that was Teddy Roosevelt era.

230
00:15:24,960 --> 00:15:25,960
He opposed that.

231
00:15:25,960 --> 00:15:27,520
Roosevelt was really opposed to that, but...

232
00:15:27,520 --> 00:15:27,820
And then what happened?

233
00:15:28,520 --> 00:15:28,900
They approved it.

234
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:30,520
Yeah.

235
00:15:30,520 --> 00:15:38,280
And, you know, people said later, like Eisenhower talking about this stuff, you know, in America we have something called the Pledge of Allegiance, which I don't think you guys probably have.

236
00:15:38,280 --> 00:15:39,280
Not in Canada.

237
00:15:40,440 --> 00:15:42,220
Obviously, you wouldn't do it to America, but to your own.

238
00:15:42,540 --> 00:15:43,240
It's kind of...

239
00:15:43,880 --> 00:15:45,080
Like a salute to the flag, right?

240
00:15:45,280 --> 00:15:46,040
Yeah, it's a little bit nationalist.

241
00:15:46,760 --> 00:15:47,760
I did it growing up.

242
00:15:47,760 --> 00:15:58,810
You put your hand over your heart and you face the flag and you say, I pledge allegiance to the flag of the United States of America and to the republic for which it stands, one nation under God, indivisible, with liberty and justice for all.

243
00:16:00,220 --> 00:16:08,730
That part about one nation under God, that was added in the 50s as a kind of a way of showing that America is not, quote unquote, a godless communist country.

244
00:16:08,920 --> 00:16:09,920
Wow.

245
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:11,630
Yeah, because communism teaches, you know, no religion.

246
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,410
And so they, make sure we're not communist.

247
00:16:15,270 --> 00:16:16,890
Okay, but we don't think that, right?

248
00:16:17,210 --> 00:16:17,450
Yeah.

249
00:16:17,590 --> 00:16:19,550
I mean, I'm all about tolerance.

250
00:16:19,790 --> 00:16:20,790
So I think that's kind of absurd.

251
00:16:21,880 --> 00:16:31,200
Okay, so what I think is really interesting, though, is that every political speech, every time a political leader has to say something publicly, he'll always quote God, right?

252
00:16:31,960 --> 00:16:32,960
Right.

253
00:16:32,960 --> 00:16:35,940
And they'll often end a speech with, you know, thank you and God bless America or something.

254
00:16:36,400 --> 00:16:36,680
Yeah, exactly.

255
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:40,520
Which, you know what, for me as a Canadian is really strange.

256
00:16:40,520 --> 00:16:48,240
Like if a Canadian president said, God bless Canada, everyone would, no one would know what to do.

257
00:16:48,240 --> 00:16:49,240
Like, I don't know.

258
00:16:49,240 --> 00:16:49,360
I don't know.

259
00:16:50,240 --> 00:16:54,360
Anyway, I had an ex-girlfriend who was British and she, like Tony Blair started with that a couple times.

260
00:16:54,360 --> 00:16:55,840
He said, you know, and God bless us all or something.

261
00:16:55,840 --> 00:16:57,240
And she was just put off by it.

262
00:16:57,280 --> 00:16:58,760
And she was like, the Brits will never take this.

263
00:16:58,760 --> 00:16:59,320
And they didn't.

264
00:17:00,420 --> 00:17:01,260
No one was impressed.

265
00:17:01,860 --> 00:17:02,200
All right.

266
00:17:02,320 --> 00:17:07,140
Well, an interesting feature of American history, politics and society.

267
00:17:07,540 --> 00:17:08,920
So thanks for that insight, Pete.

268
00:17:09,240 --> 00:17:09,400
Cool.

269
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:09,860
My pleasure.

270
00:17:10,120 --> 00:17:10,260
Yeah.

